// Language section: Imports & Modules.
page imports { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Imports & Modules" {}

  text {
    span "An `import` pulls another file's declarations into the current document. There are two forms, distinguished by how the path is written." {}
  }

  h2 "Disk imports" {}

  text {
    span "A quoted path imports a file from disk, resolved relative to the importing file." {}
  }
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
import "./pages/values.wcl"
import "../shared/types.wcl"
WCL
  }

  h2 "System imports" {}

  text {
    span "An angle-bracket path imports a **system** module — a file provided by the host program through a registry, not the filesystem. wdoc's standard library is served this way." {}
  }
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
import <wdoc/prelude.wcl>
WCL
  }

  callout "wdoc auto-imports its prelude" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "When you build a wdoc site, the build prepends import <wdoc/prelude.wcl> for you, so page / text / code / callout and the rest of the stdlib are already in scope. You only write imports for your own page files."
  }

  h2 "How imports compose" {}

  text {
    span "Top-level imports are eager; an `import` inside a block is lazy. Imported declarations participate fully in the importer: they take part in structural validation and in name resolution, so a type or `let` declared in an imported file is usable as if it were local." {}
  }

  text {
    span "Hosts that drive WCL can also tag each block with its source file, which lets tooling order imported-versus-local output (wdoc uses this for CSS cascade, for instance)." {}
  }

  text {
    span "Move on to the [Reference section's Builtin Functions](builtins)." {}
  }
}
